gh investigation, since the plan mode Phase 1 says to use Explore agents. But that might be overkill and slow. Let me do a mix: a few direct reads + maybe one Explore agent for the .collab and git tracking question.\n\nActually, given the constraints and that I want to be efficient, let me do direct reads of specific files that are named, in parallel. Then a couple of bash commands for git tracking and environment.\n\nLet me think about what's most valuable to verify:\n\n1. TB-6 is the most consequential and verifiable: Is `.collab/` tracked by git? Is it in .gitignore? Does AC-025 require npm pack to exclude it? If `.collab/` contains the only copy of the authoritative spec (interfaces.md, B.4.4 goldens, review-v1 verdict), and it's untracked, then a clean CI checkout can't run T-004/AC-016/AC-010/AC-015/AC-067. This is a strong blocking finding.\n\n2. TB-1 about the injectable clock: the design says \"固定时钟\" (fixed clock) in T-003 but many ACs need advanceable time without sleep. This is a real design inconsistency. Verifiable from the design text itself.\n\n3. TB-2 about AC-038 baseline=smoke vs levels=[integration], WSL not on CI runners, no wsl-discover step in flows. Real.\n\n4. TB-3 about M1 title \"修复 A 节全部缺陷\" vs defects assigned to M2/M4/M5. Real.\n\n5. TB-4 about sha256 frozen-files covering live lib vs fixture copy. Real - critical because check-fast runs on every check.\n\n6. TB-5 about traceability merge semantics and T-030 depends_on. Real.\n\nThese are all legitimate. As reviewer, I'd incorporate them as findings. Let me also think about whether I can find additional issues the test architect missed, to add value as a reviewer.\n\nPotential additional findings:\n- The design says \"冻结后只许加字段，不许改\" (after freeze only add fields, not change) but also says snapshot_version must increment on new codes. There's a subtle tension: adding a field to registry requires snapshot update, which is fine. But \"只许加字段，不许改\" (only add fields, not change) — yet AC-010 says \"新增项未进快照：失败\" and \"snapshot_version 没有递增：失败\". Consistent.\n\n- Section 0.6: \"第 i 个租约的 expires_at = selected_at + lease_ttl_s + i × timeout_s\" — but AC-064 says \"count=3 时，在注入时钟下按顺序执行，三个租约都没有过期\". With the formula, the later leases have LATER expiry, so executing in order is fine. But there's a subtlety: if you execute calls[0], calls[1], calls[2] sequentially, each takes up to timeout_s. By the time you get to calls[i], elapsed ≈ i×timeout_s. Lease i expires at selected_at + lease_ttl_s + i×timeout_s. So you need lease_ttl_s ≥ elapsed overhead. Works if execution is tight. OK.\n\n- A potential issue: the design's `E_ADMIN_REQUIRED` (section 0.5, 6) vs `E_VIEW_FORBIDDEN` (section 6, AC-059, AC-056). Section 0.5 says management routes require loopback session token \"否则返回 E_ADMIN_REQUIRED\". Section 6 says select with view:full without session returns \"403 E_VIEW_FORBIDDEN\". AC-059 says \"带 view 的 select 都返回 403 和 E_ADMIN_REQUIRED 或 E_VIEW_FORBIDDEN\" — so it's ambiguous which code applies where. This is an inconsistency in error code assignment — minor/major. Actually this matters because the contract is frozen in M1 (T-004) and error codes must be uniquely defined. If AC-059 allows two different codes for the same condition, that's a \"每条 AC 只有唯一预期值\" (F-03) violation. This is a real finding I can add.\n\n- Section 0.5 mentions `E_ADMIN_REQUIRED`; section 5 mentions the registry should contain all error codes. But section 6/AC-060 introduces `E_READONLY`, `E_CSRF`; AC-058 introduces `E_LEASE_CONSUMED`, `E_PROVIDER_AUTH`, `E_PROVIDER_QUOTA`, `E_TIMEOUT`, `E_LEASE_EXPIRED`, `E_LEASE_CONFIG_MISMATCH`; AC-061 `E_HEALTH_DEGRADED`; AC-069 `E_SMOKE_DISABLED`; AC-013 `E_CONFIG_EXISTS`; AC-014 `E_CONFIG`; AC-033 `E_CONFIG_SECRET_INLINE`, `E_SECRET_BACKEND`; AC-026 `E_UNSAFE_ARG`; AC-029 `E_STATE_LOCKED`; AC-065... The registry \"一次性登记 interfaces 第 5 节的全部码与枚举\" — but interfaces.md is in .collab/ (untracked). And many codes are scattered across ACs. T-004 inputs say \"interfaces 第 1～15 节\". So completeness of the frozen registry depends on an untracked doc. This reinforces TB-6.\n\n- Another: AC-023 says \"除 run 外，每个 CLI 命令的完整 stdout 都能被 JSON.parse 解析为单个值\" — but AC-057 says \"run --lease 的 stdout 每行都通过 event/v1 校验\" (streaming, multiple lines). OK consistent.\n\n- Potential issue: `forecast/v1 新增 tasks_left_basis、risk_basis` (F-14) is a \"semantic_change\" — but the contract is frozen in M1 at T-004. If these fields are added in M1's registry, then that's fine. But F-14 says forecast/v1 \"新增\" these — if forecast/v1 already existed in v0.1, adding fields to a frozen schema is \"加字段\" which is allowed. OK.\n\n- The design says contract freeze in M1 with \"后续任务不得修改注册表\" but F-14/AC-067 and T-014 (config v2 migration) and T-006 (forecast engine) all need to introduce new semantics/codes. The design handles this by putting everything in T-004. But there's a question of whether T-004 can really enumerate ALL codes including ones discovered during M2-M5 implementation (e.g., E_CSRF, E_PROVIDER_QUOTA, E_SMOKE_DISABLED). If a new code is discovered later, \"走蓝图变更，并递增 snapshot_version\" — that's a heavy process but defined. OK, this is actually addressed.\n\n- Section 6 \"会话令牌由 GET /cliworker/api/session 签发\" — but /cliworker/api/* is listed as 管理级 requiring x-cliworker-session. So to GET a session token, you need... a session token? Circular dependency! Let me re-read.\n\n\"管理级 | ... /cliworker/api/* ... | 需要 x-cliworker-session\" and \"会话令牌由 GET /cliworker/api/session 签发，只对 loopback 来源签发\". So `/cliworker/api/session` is under `/cliworker/api/*` which requires a session token to access, but it's the endpoint that ISSUES the token. This is a bootstrapping/circular-auth problem. AC-059 says \"伪造非 loopback 来源请求 session 时返回 403\" — implies session endpoint is reachable without a token (you request it to get a token). So there's an inconsistency: either /api/session is exempted from the session requirement (needs explicit definition), or it's unreachable. This is a real, specific finding I can add. Good catch.\n\nLet me verify: \"写接口还需要 x-cliworker-csrf\" — so session issuance is a GET (read), no csrf. But it still needs to be exempted from x-cliworker-session requirement. The design doesn't state this exemption explicitly. AC-059: \"loopback 会话可以访问\" and \"伪造非 loopback 来源请求 session 时返回 403\". So the rule is: session endpoint checks loopback source, issues token. But the route table says /cliworker/api/* requires x-cliworker-session. Contradiction. This is a legit finding.\n\n- Another: Section 5 \"值的禁用表在运行时由当前目录生成（模型 id、key、CLI 可执行名）\" and AC-011 \"禁用值按切词后的整词匹配目录里的模型 id、key 和 CLI 可执行名\". But `call` subtree \"必须逐项等于包装模板\" — the wrapper template contains the CLI executable name and possibly model key in args. So the value scan is excluded from call but the template equality check requires exact match. OK, that's defined.\n\n- AC-011 canary: \"cliworker、config、dsh-CLIWorker 不会误报\" — but \"dsh-CLIWorker\" contains hyphens; word-splitting on `[^A-Za-z0-9._-]` keeps `-`, so \"dsh-CLIWorker\" is one token. If a forbidden value is \"cliworker\" (lowercase), would \"dsh-CLIWorker\" match? The match is \"整词匹配\" (whole word match) — \"dsh-CLIWorker\" as a single token is not equal to \"cliworker\", so no false positive. OK. But \"CLI 可执行名\" like \"codex\" — if a model id or path contains \"codex\"... hmm. Actually the forbidden VALUES are model ids, keys, CLI executable names. These are being scanned for in the AgentLine view. If the AgentLine view legitimately contains, say, an error code that happens to equal a key... edge case. Let me not go too deep.\n\nActually wait — there's a subtle issue with value scanning: forbidden values are \"模型 id、key、CLI 可执行名\" generated at runtime from the current catalog. But the whole point of AgentLine view is that it must NOT leak provider/model details. If forbidden values are generated from the catalog, and the catalog itself can contain arbitrary keys (manual entry via `models add`, AC-041), then a user could add a model whose id is a common word like \"ok\" or \"select\" — and then the scanner would flag legitimate fields. Conversely, if forbidden values are only the ones in the current catalog, then a model present in the view but whose key was generated differently... Actually the scanner should catch any model id. Hmm, this is getting complex. Let me not over-engineer; I'll focus on the clear, high-value findings.\n\nLet me also reconsider: maybe the reviewer's job is specifically to evaluate the ROUND-2 DESIGN against the PREVIOUS review findings F-01..F-16, and the TB blockers are given as input from the test architect. The findings I output should be about the design's remaining problems. The most defensible approach: output findings that correspond to the 6 TB blockers (as blocking/major), plus a couple of my own, with verdict \"revise\".\n\nNow let me think about the severity and status:\n- All findings are \"open\" (they're problems I'm raising).\n- Severity: TB-1 (clock) blocking, TB-2 (baseline/release-gate) blocking, TB-3 (M1 title) major, TB-4 (frozen-files hash) blocking, TB-5 (traceability merge/deps) major, TB-6 (untracked spec) blocking.\n- Plus my own: session bootstrapping circular auth (blocking or major), E_ADMIN_REQUIRED vs E_VIEW_FORBIDDEN ambiguity (major, since F-03 requires unique expected values and contract is frozen).\n\nShould I verify things with actual file reads? I think yes — to be a credible reviewer and to ground finding TB-6 especially. Let me do a batch of read-only operations.\n\nLet me do this:\n1. Read the directory listing / key files.\n2. Check git tracking of .collab/.\n3. Check .gitignore, lib/config.mjs, package.json.\n\nLet me use parallel tool calls. Note: in plan mode, read-only bash is OK.\n\nLet me first check what files exist.\n\nActually, I need to be careful: the working directory is c:\\git\\dsh-CLIWorker. Let me check.\n\nLet me run several read-only commands in parallel:\n- `git ls-files | head -100` and `git ls-files .collab` \n- `git status --short`\n- Read `.gitignore`\n- Read `lib/config.mjs`\n- Read `lib/select.mjs`\n- Read `package.json`\n- Glob `.collab/blueprint/cliworker-v2/**`\n\nLet me do it. I'll batch: a Bash for git, and Reads/Globs.\n\nActually the tool guidance says prefer Glob over find/ls, Grep over grep, Read over cat. Let me use:\n- Glob for `**/*` to see structure (or specific)\n- Grep / Read for specific files\n- Bash for `git ls-files`, `git check-ignore`, `node --version`, `pwsh --version`\n\nLet me do a first batch of parallel calls.\n\nI'll be efficient. Let me make these calls:\n\n1. Bash: `git ls-files | grep -E '\\.collab|interfaces|blueprint' ; echo \"---status---\"; git status --short | head -40; echo \"---ignore---\"; git check-ignore -v .collab/blueprint/cliworker-v2/interfaces.md 2>&1 || echo \"NOT IGNORED\"` — hmm, but I should use Grep tool instead of grep in bash. Actually for git output, bash piping is fine. Let me just use plain `git ls-files` and `git status`.\n\nLet me simplify: \n- Bash: `git ls-files | sed -n '1,120p'` — hmm sed is discouraged (Edit tool). Actually for reading command output, a pipe to sed/head is fine since it's not file editing. But guidance says don't use cat/head/tail for reading files. For command output it's OK. Let me just use `git ls-files`.\n\nLet me do:\n- Bash: `git ls-files`\n- Bash: `git status --short`\n- Bash: `git check-ignore -v config/a.local.json state/x a.jsonl config/cliworker.example.json 2>&1; echo EXIT=$?`\n- Read: `.gitignore`\n- Read: `lib/config.mjs`\n- Read: `lib/select.mjs`\n- Read: `package.json`\n- Glob: `.collab/**`\n\nThat's a lot but they're independent. Let me batch them.\n\nActually, let me be a bit more targeted and not overwhelm. The core question for my verdict is: are the TB blockers valid? Most are derivable purely from the design text (which I have in full). Only TB-6 needs codebase verification (is .collab tracked?). TB-4 needs co