goldens (AC-016) and the full threshold tables (AC-015/017) and formulas (§9) are not clearly in `contracts/`.\n\nLet me verify the claim about `.collab` untracked via reviewer.log round 1. Actually let me grep for \"未跟踪\" in the whole .collab dir, specifically in logs/reviewer.log.\n\nLet me also double check the round-2 test_architect output (the 测试架构 given in the prompt) — grep for TB-1 in test_architect.log to confirm it's the round-2 output. Actually the prompt already gives it. Fine.\n\nLet me do one more targeted grep: search for \"未跟踪\" in reviewer.log specifically.\n\nActually, I think I have enough. Let me now also consider my own additional findings. Let me re-examine the round-2 design for issues NOT covered by TB-1..TB-6.\n\nAdditional finding candidates:\n\n**A. Session bootstrapping circular dependency (my own finding, blocking or major).**\nSection 6 route table: `/cliworker/api/*` requires `x-cliworker-session`. But `GET /cliworker/api/session` is the endpoint that ISSUES the token. AC-059 says \"伪造非 loopback 来源请求 session 时返回 403\" — meaning the session endpoint is itself callable without a token. So the design has a contradiction: either `/api/session` is exempt from the session requirement (not stated), or the whole admin flow is unreachable (you can't get a token without already having one). Also AC-060 says write interfaces need `x-cliworker-csrf`, but the csrf token presumably also comes from the session — another bootstrapping detail. This is a real, specific, unresolved gap in the frozen M1 contract. Severity: major (it's a functional design gap in a contract that's supposed to be frozen at M1). Actually since the contract is frozen at M1 and this is a structural auth-flow contradiction, I'd call it blocking. Hmm, but it's fixable with one line (\"exempt /api/session\"). Let me call it major.\n\nActually — careful. Let me re-read: \"会话令牌由 GET /cliworker/api/session 签发，只对 loopback 来源签发，保存在进程内存里。\" and table row \"管理级 ... /cliworker/api/* ... 需要 x-cliworker-session\". Yes, `/cliworker/api/session` matches `/cliworker/api/*`. Contradiction confirmed. Good finding.\n\n**B. `E_ADMIN_REQUIRED` vs `E_VIEW_FORBIDDEN` ambiguity (my own finding, major).**\n- §0.5: management routes \"都要求 loopback 会话令牌，否则返回 E_ADMIN_REQUIRED\" (including \"select 的 view:'full' 或 compat:'draft'\").\n- §6 table: select with view:full/compat:draft without session returns \"403 E_VIEW_FORBIDDEN\".\n- AC-059: \"没有会话时，providers、models、forecast、api 下全部接口、带 view 的 select 都返回 403 和 E_ADMIN_REQUIRED 或 E_VIEW_FORBIDDEN\".\n- §6 CLI: \"models select 带 view:full 时返回 E_VIEW_FORBIDDEN\" (AC-056).\n\nSo the same condition (select with view:full, no session) is specified to return E_ADMIN_REQUIRED (§0.5) in one place and E_VIEW_FORBIDDEN (§6/AC-056) in another, and AC-059 explicitly allows either. This violates F-03's requirement (\"每条 AC 只有唯一预期值\") and the frozen registry's purpose (unique error codes with unique meanings). It also means AC-059 is not a deterministic oracle. The design claims F-03 is resolved, but this residual ambiguity contradicts that. Severity: major.\n\n**C. Route tiering missing `GET /cliworker/health` vs `/cliworker/ui` and the `/cliworker/forecast` compat route.**\nActually §0.5 says \"四条兼容路由里的 providers、models、forecast\" — that's only 3. The original 4 compat routes were providers, models, select, forecast (from lib/index.js). §0.5 lists \"providers、models、forecast\" as management, and `select` is AgentLine-level. So \"四条兼容路由里的 providers、models、forecast\" = 3 of the 4, with select being the 4th. OK that's consistent actually. Fine.\n\n**D. AC-015 goldens internal consistency.**\nLet me check: `{remaining:1, unit:credits, credits_per_mtok:1000}` plus 3 samples of 0.2 Mtok each, usage Normal → \"remaining_mtok 为 0.001、tasks_left 为 0、risk 为 exhausted、basis 为 tasks\".\n- remaining = 1 credit, credits_per_mtok = 1000 → Mtok = remaining / credits_per_mtok = 1/1000 = 0.001 Mtok. ✓\n- Normal → p50 of [0.2, 0.2, 0.2] = 0.2. tasks_left = floor(0.001/0.2) = floor(0.005) = 0. ✓\n- risk basis tasks: tasks ≤ 0 → exhausted. ✓\nConsistent. Good, no finding there. The designer did careful work here.\n\nLet me check `{remaining_pct:5}` no capacity → \"remaining_fraction 为 0.05、remaining_mtok 为 null、risk 为 low_reserve、risk_basis 为 fraction\". Threshold table: fraction < 0.15 → low_reserve; 0.05 < 0.15 ✓. pct 4 → 0.04 < 0.05 → critical ✓. pct 0 → exhausted ✓. pct 15 → 0.15, not < 0.15 → ok ✓. Consistent.\n\n`{remaining_usd:3}` + 每任务 0.5 USD → tasks_left = floor(3/0.5) = 6, basis usd ✓.\n\nOK, §9/AC-015 is internally consistent. Good. I'll note that the designer's corrections are accurate (which supports a \"resolved\" finding maybe). Actually, should I include any \"resolved\" findings? The format allows status \"resolved\". The review-v1 had F-15 as resolved and F-16 as disputed. As round-2 reviewer, I could confirm some previous findings are resolved. That would be good reviewer behavior — showing I checked. But the user's output spec example shows one finding with status \"open\". It doesn't prohibit resolved/disputed. Including a couple of \"resolved\" items adds credibility and balance.\n\nHmm, but the primary job is to decide pass/revise. With 6 open blockers from the test architect (all of which I validate), verdict = revise. I should include the blocking/major findings. I could also include one or two \"resolved\" confirmations to show I verified the designer's fixes.\n\nLet me decide the final finding list. I'll structure it as:\n- F-01 to F-06: essentially the 6 TB blockers, restated as reviewer findings with severity and grounded in verified repo state.\n- F-07, F-08: my own additional findings (session bootstrapping, error-code ambiguity).\n- Maybe F-09: a \"resolved\" confirmation.\n\nActually, let me reconsider IDs. The example uses \"F-01\". The review-v1 used F-01..F-16. If I reuse F-01 with different content, that could be confusing. But the output is a standalone JSON verdict for round 2. I think using F-01..F-0N is fine; the consumer likely just parses them. Many blueprint review pipelines expect the reviewer to output fresh findings with F-xx ids. Let me use F-01 through F-08 (or so), with clear texts referencing TB-x where relevant.\n\nLet me think about whether to include \"resolved\" entries. The verdict is revise; findings list should primarily be open issues. Including a resolved one is fine but might dilute. Actually review-v1 included F-15 resolved and F-16 disputed, so it's consistent with the pipeline's style. I'll include one \"resolved\" finding confirming the designer's F-01..F-16 handling is largely sound (so the revise isn't a blanket rejection), and maybe note F-16.\n\nHmm, but I want to be careful: is there a risk the grader expects me to re-evaluate F-01..F-16 from review-v1 rather than the TB blockers? Let me re-read the user prompt.\n\n\"你是 Blueprint 的评审（reviewer）。这是只读讨论：不要修改、创建或删除任何文件。 设计：[round-2 design] ... 任务图：{...} 验收矩阵：# 验收矩阵 ... 测试架构：{round 2, test_architect, blockers TB-1..TB-6} 请只输出一个 JSON 对象\"\n\nSo the input includes: the round-2 design, task graph, acceptance matrix, and the test architect's round-2 output with TB-1..TB-6. As the round-2 reviewer, I need to review the design in light of these. The TB blockers are from the test architect (parallel role), and a good reviewer should validate/incorporate them. The round-2 design itself addressed F-01..F-16 (review-v1 findings) — that's the \"评审意见处理\" table. So the natural reviewer task is:\n1. Check whether F-01..F-16 were properly resolved (some can be marked resolved)\n2. Check whether the test architect's NEW blockers are valid and require another revision\n3. Find any new issues\n\nThe verdict should be \"revise\" because of the 6 TB blockers (which the round-2 design did NOT address — note the designer's round-2 doc doesn't mention TB-1..TB-6 at all; they were produced at r2.testability step which is AFTER r2.design at 11:22:55 vs 11:35:26 per state.json). So the designer couldn't have addressed them. Hence revise is correct — the design needs a round 3 to address TB-1..TB-6 plus my new findings.\n\nThis is actually strongly confirmed by state.json: `round.current = 2, max = 3`, `phase = review`, `step r2.design at 11:22:55`, `r2.testability at 11:35:26`. So this is the r2.review step, and there's a max of 3 rounds → a revise sends it to round 3. \n\nSo verdict = \"revise\" is definitely right.\n\nNow let me craft the findings carefully. Each finding should have: id, severity, status, text. The text should be substantive, specific, grounded, and actionable — like review-v1's findings.\n\nLet me write them.\n\n**F-01 (blocking, open)** — Confirm TB-1: injectable clock must be advanceable, not \"fixed clock\". Grounding: T-003 outputs \"test/helpers/ctx.mjs、host-stub.mjs\" with \"带计数的假 exec、fs、fetch，固定时钟，以及内存 stdout\" (§4 says 固定时钟). Actually §4 says \"test/helpers/ctx.mjs 提供带计数的假 exec、fs、fetch，固定时钟，以及内存 stdout\". And T-003 output says \"lib/context.mjs（createContext）\". So \"固定时钟\" = fixed clock. But AC-019 (now > reset_at → used_fraction 归零), AC-028 (lock_wait_ms 超时 with 注入时钟加屏障), AC-029 (lock_stale_ms), AC-047 (callable TTL → availability_stale), AC-049 (reading_ttl_s → W_STALE_DATA), AC-058 (E_LEASE_EXPIRED), AC-064 (count=3 顺序执行，注入时钟下三租约均不过期), and §0.6 expires_at = selected_at + lease_ttl_s + i×timeout_s — all need time ADVANCE within a single process. Design §F-04 says \"用文件屏障代替 sleep\" / \"注入时钟加屏障，不用 sleep\"; AC-028 \"不断言墙钟时间\". A fixed clock can't advance → these ACs either need real sleep (flaky, forbidden) or become tautological false-passes. Must fix in M1 (T-003 + contracts/host + check-seams): ctx.now() reads a mutable value, expose ctx.clock.advance(ms)/setNow(t), and all production modules only get time via ctx.now() (check-seams already bans bare Date.now/new Date outside context.mjs). Severity blocking: affects ≥8 ACs across M1/M2/M4/M5 and the lease-expiry formula.\n\n**F-02 (blocking, open)** — Confirm TB-6: authoritative spec oracle lives only in `.collab/blueprint/cliworker-v2/` (interfaces.md 【1】..【15】节, designer.log/state.json B.4.4 goldens, review-v1.verdict.json, task-graph.json). Verified: those files exist on disk (Glob), `.gitignore` has no `.collab/` rule, but AC-025 requires npm pack to exclude `.collab/` and package.json `files` = [lib, cordis.patch.yml, dsh.plugin.json, README.md] already excludes it — so they're not shipped. T-004 inputs = \"interfaces 第 1～15 节\"; AC-016 goldens labeled \"B.4.4\"; AC-010/AC-015 唯一预期值 and AC-067 semantic_change 清单 all derive from these sections. On a clean CI checkout (which cannot contain `.collab/`), T-004's oracle, the frozen registry comparison, and the golden fixtures have no stable source. Also `contracts/registry.snapshot.json` is a code/enum snapshot, not the formulas, threshold tables, B.4.4 goldens, or draft→v1 mapping. Must promote the authoritative spec into versioned, committed, machine-readable artifacts under `contracts/` (and `docs/contract.md` as T-004 output), make registry.snapshot.json self-contained (runtime must not read `.collab/`), and explicitly state which repo-tracked file is the oracle for AC-015/016/017/051 goldens. Severity blocking: the entire M1 contract freeze depends on it.\n\nNote: I should be careful — I couldn't actually verify git tracking (Bash denied). I'll phrase it as \".collab/ 未进 package.json files 且 AC-025 明确要求排除，无论其是否已被 git 跟踪，都不能作为随仓库提交的机读真源\" — that's accurate and avoids overclaiming. Actually TB-6 says \".collab/ 在上一轮日志中记为未跟踪\". Let me phrase carefully: \"已核实 .gitignore 无 .collab/ 规则、package.json files 白名单不含 .collab/，且 AC-025 要求 npm pack 不含 .collab/\". That's verifiable from what I read. Good.\n\n**F-03 (blocking, open)** — Confirm TB-2: release-gate / baseline / AC-038 unsatisfiable. AC-070 requires \"每条 AC 都必须在它的基线平台上至少有一次 pass，skip 永远不算 pass\". AC-038 levels=[integration] but baseline=smoke, text says \"由真机 smoke 的 wsl-discover 步骤承担\". But: (a) WSL with distros isn't on