ingle catch-all \"resolved\" F-09. Maybe I do: F-01..F-08 re-evaluated (resolved for the 8? no...). Hmm.\n\nHold on. Let me reconsider whether the 8 findings are actually resolved, given the TBs map onto them:\n- TB-1 → residual gap in F-01's fix (file clock)\n- TB-2 → residual gap in F-02's fix (golden integrity)\n- TB-3 → residual gap in F-06's fix (full-run set equality with skips)\n- TB-4 → residual gap in F-05's fix (multi-owner defect)\n- TB-5 → residual gap in F-04's fix (repro integrity)\n- TB-6 → residual gap in F-02/F-06's fix (ac-index ownership)\n\nSo ALL 6 TBs are residual gaps in the round-3 fixes of the 8 findings. Interesting! So one could argue that the 8 findings are NOT fully resolved because their fixes have holes. \n\nHmm, this makes the picture: the round-3 design made substantive progress on all 8, but each fix has a residual hole that the test architect caught. So a fair reviewer verdict: \"revise\", with findings being the 6 TBs (confirmed) — these ARE the open issues. And the 8 original findings: I'd say they're \"substantially addressed but with residual gaps\" — I could mark them \"disputed\"? No.\n\nActually, you know what — I bet the cleanest and most expected output is: **findings = the 6 confirmed TB blockers, with fresh IDs F-01..F-06 (or keeping the TB mapping like review-v2 did: \"确认 TB-1\")**, plus maybe 1-2 new findings I discover myself, plus a \"resolved\" catch-all acknowledgment. verdict = \"revise\".\n\nLet me look at how review-v2 worded findings: \"确认 TB-1：...\" — the reviewer CONFIRMED the test architect's blockers and made them findings with F-01..F-06 IDs. F-07/F-08 were \"新增\" (reviewer's own). F-09 was \"已核验、无需改动\" (resolved).\n\nSo for round 3, the analogous: I confirm TB-1..TB-6 → F-01..F-06 (or F-01..F-06 mapping to TB-1..TB-6 in order, like v2 did with \"确认 TB-1\" for F-01). Then add my own new findings (F-07, F-08, ...) if I find any. Then possibly a resolved catch-all.\n\nGiven the v2 pattern, I'll do:\n- F-01..F-06: confirm TB-1..TB-6 (in order), with severities.\n- Plus perhaps new findings from my own analysis.\n\nBut wait — should I confirm all 6 TBs? As a good reviewer, I should verify each. Let me examine TB-3 and TB-6 more carefully, and check whether some TBs might be \"disputed\" (i.e., I disagree). Let me be rigorous. If I disagree with a TB, I'd mark it disputed or not include it. Let me examine.\n\n**TB-3 (full-run set equality vs all-skipped files)**: \nAC-001: \"run-tests 枚举 test 下 unit、integration、contract、e2e、smoke、characterization、flows 中的 *.test.mjs...完整运行写 files-executed.json（mode:'full'），并当场断言枚举集合与 reporter 实际执行集合相等\". \nThe set equality is between \"枚举集合\" (enumerated set) and \"reporter 实际执行集合\" (reporter's actually-executed set). If a file's all tests are skipped, does the reporter count it as \"executed\"? In Node's test runner, a file with all skipped tests is still \"loaded\" and reported (with skip counts). But \"实际执行集合\" (actually executed) could be interpreted as files with at least one executed test. The design doesn't define it. \n\nThe concern: on ubuntu/macos, AC-027 (win32 baseline) writes a skip record; AC-033 native backends platform-gated; AC-038 dry-run... Actually wait, AC-038 is smoke level; smoke tests run on which OS? The CI matrix has ubuntu, windows, macos. smoke tests — §6 says smoke can be linux (dry-run) or real-smoke. AC-069 dry-run smoke runs in CI. AC-038 real-smoke only on maintainer's machine. So on ubuntu CI, AC-038's smoke test would be... skipped? Or written as a skip record. The design says \"AC-038 ... dry-run 报告永远不能满足 real-smoke\" and \"smoke 报告里 wsl-discover 步骤\". Hmm, is there a test file `test/smoke/wsl-discover.test.mjs` that T-029 outputs (\"test/smoke/wsl-discover.test.mjs（报告条目校验）\")? Yes. That test validates report entries — it can run on any OS with fixtures. So maybe no all-skipped file.\n\nBut AC-027 (win32 baseline): \"其他平台写跳过记录\". If the test file `test/integration/exec-native.test.mjs` has a single test that's skipped on non-win32, then the file is \"loaded\" but zero tests executed → \"reporter 实际执行集合\" might exclude it → set inequality → full run fails on ubuntu/macos. That's a real risk. AC-002 says skips.jsonl is uploaded separately, but doesn't define whether files-executed includes all-skipped files.\n\nTB-3 is a legitimate finding: ambiguous definition of \"reporter 实际执行集合\" + no canary for all-skipped files + 3-OS matrix makes it unimplementable as literally stated. I'd confirm it. Severity: major (it's a test-infrastructure correctness issue that would block CI on 2 of 3 OSes). Could be blocking. Hmm.\n\nActually, is it blocking? It would cause the full `npm test` to fail on ubuntu/macos — that's a hard failure of AC-001 which is itself the runner. That's pretty fundamental. But it's fixable by a one-sentence clarification (\"files-executed records loaded files, skips only go to skips.jsonl\"). I'd say major, maybe blocking. Let me consider the overall: I'll assign severities across 6 findings: a mix of blocking and major, maybe one minor. \n\n**TB-6 (ac-index ownership)**: verified — §0 says all contracts/spec/ files written by T-004 including AC index; §4 says ac-index by T-001; task graph T-001 write_scope has ac-index, T-004 doesn't. Real contradiction. This is a blueprint-level inconsistency that must be fixed (single source of truth). Severity: major. Actually — hmm, is it really \"these files all by T-004\"? Let me re-read §0.2: \"contracts/spec/ 放 AC 索引、保留面与语义变更面、迁移映射；这些文件都由 T-004 在 M1 写入并提交，实现任务只读不写。\" Yes — \"这些文件\" = all the contracts/spec/ files just listed = AC index, surfaces, migration map. So §0 assigns ac-index to T-004. §4 and task graph assign it to T-001. Confirmed contradiction. \n\nBut wait — T-001 is also in M1 and is an \"implementation task\"? §0 says \"实现任务只读不写\" (implementation tasks read-only, don't write). T-001 IS an implementation task (it writes scripts). If T-001 writes ac-index.json, that contradicts \"实现任务只读不写\" too! Actually §0's statement is about spec files: \"这些文件都由 T-004 在 M1 写入并提交，实现任务只读不写\" — meaning the spec files are written by T-004 (which is itself an M1 task, so \"实现任务\" here means tasks other than T-004?). Hmm, T-004 is also an implementation task. The phrase is confusing. Anyway, the contradiction is real: §0 gives ac-index to T-004, §4/task-graph give it to T-001. Both are M1 tasks. This must be pinned. Confirmed, major.\n\nNow, are there additional issues I should find myself (beyond the 6 TBs)? Let me do my own consistency check on the task graph and acceptance matrix. Let me look for:\n\n1. **AC-074 / milestone-gate / check-defects with multi-owner a1-quota** — TB-4 covers.\n\n2. **Check the acceptance matrix: AC-038 baseline real-smoke, but §6 says \"smoke 只能取 linux（dry-run）或 real-smoke\"** — OK. But wait: AC-069 has levels=[smoke] and baseline? It should be linux (dry-run). Let me check AC-069 text: \"smoke-real --dry-run ... 生成通过 cliworker.smoke/v1 校验的报告（mode dry-run）\". No explicit baseline in text; baseline comes from ac-index. The design says baseline only declared in ac-index. Fine.\n\n3. **AC-001/AC-004/AC-073 and the 74 ACs**: ac-index must contain AC-001..AC-074. Let me count the acceptance entries in the task graph: AC-001 through AC-074. Let me verify there are exactly 74 and all are in the matrix. The matrix lists AC-001..AC-074. Let me count from the matrix... it goes 001-074 sequentially. Let me verify a few potentially problematic ones:\n   - AC-038 → T-029 (matrix says T-029) ✓\n   - AC-069 → T-029 ✓\n   - AC-072 → T-003 ✓\n   - AC-073 → T-004 ✓\n   - AC-074 → T-001 ✓\n   \n   Hmm wait, there's a subtle issue: F-02's §0 says \"这些文件都由 T-004 在 M1 写入\" but AC-073 requires \"contracts/spec/ac-index.json...都已被 git 跟踪...并通过各自的 schema 校验\" — checked by T-004's test `test/contract/spec-tracked.test.mjs`. But ac-index is written by T-001. T-004 depends_on T-001? Let me check: T-004 depends_on [\"T-001\"] ✓. OK good, so T-004 can read ac-index written by T-001. Fine.\n\n   But another: T-001's AC-004 validates \"contracts/spec/ac-index.json 恰含 AC-001..AC-074\" — T-001 writes it and validates it. Fine.\n\n4. **T-030 depends_on**: F-06 demanded \"T-030 直接依赖所有产出流程步骤的任务\". T-030 depends_on: T-001, T-012, T-013, T-016, T-017, T-020, T-022, T-023, T-024, T-029. AC-071's 9 flow steps: auth (T-013), doctor (T-012/T-024), discover (T-016), enumerate (T-017), probe (T-020), select (T-022/T-007/T-012), run (T-023), http-select (T-024), http-admin (T-024). Let me check: select step — T-012 writes \"test/flows/steps/select.mjs（最小版）\", T-022 replaces it with v1 version. T-030 depends on T-022 but NOT T-012... wait, T-030 depends_on includes T-012 ✓. OK. What about T-025 (ui)? Not a flow step. T-027? Not a flow step (but T-029 depends on T-027). T-026? Not a flow step, but T-029 depends on T-026. OK. \n\n   Hmm, what about the flow step \"doctor\"? T-012 writes \"test/flows/steps/doctor.mjs（最小版）\", T-024 writes \"test/flows/steps/doctor.mjs（完整版）\". T-030 depends on T-024 ✓ and T-012 ✓. OK.\n\n   So T-030's depends_on seems to cover all flow-step producers. Good. F-06's dependency concern is addressed.\n\n5. **AC-059's table**: \"GET api/session 与 GET ui 在非 loopback 远端、非 loopback Host、非 loopback Origin 三种伪造下各返回 403 E_LOOPBACK_ONLY\". But §7's loopback check says \"Origin 不存在，或也是 loopback\" — so a non-loopback Origin → fail. OK. But here's a subtle issue: the three forgery cases for session/ui produce E_LOOPBACK_ONLY. But §7 route table says these are the ONLY routes that check loopback. What about `GET /cliworker/health` (公共)? No loopback check mentioned. OK.\n\n   Now — F-08 demanded \"每个触发条件只对应一个错误码\" and \"把两码以不同触发条件写进 registry.snapshot.json\". Round-3: E_VIEW_FORBIDDEN for select with view:full/draft without session; E_ADMIN_REQUIRED for management reads without session; E_LOOPBACK_ONLY for session/ui non-loopback; E_READONLY, E_CSRF. AC-009: \"E_VIEW_FORBIDDEN、E_LOOPBACK_ONLY、E_ADMIN_REQUIRED、E_READONLY、E_CSRF 的触发互斥，写路由判定顺序登记为会话、readonly、csrf、请求体\". Hmm — but the 5 codes' mutual exclusivity: E_ADMIN_REQUIRED appears both in management reads (no session) AND write step 1 (session invalid). Different triggers but same code — is that OK? \"每个码带唯一的 trigger_id\" and \"不存在两个码共用一个触发条件\". Two different triggers mapping to the same code is fine as long as each trigger has one code. But \"E_ADMIN_REQUIRED\" with two distinct trigger conditions might violate \"每个码带唯一的 trigger_id\" if trigger_id is per-code. Hmm, AC-009 says \"每个码都有 ... trigger_id、trigger，trigger_id 全表唯一\" — \"全表唯一\" means unique across the table. If E_ADMIN_REQUIRED has two triggers, it'd need two trigger_ids or one trigger_id covering both. This is a potential ambiguity but minor. Actually, the write route order step 1 \"会话无效：E_ADMIN_REQUIRED\" and management read \"没有有效会话返回 403 E_ADMIN_REQUIRED\" — these are arguably the same trigger condition (invalid/missing session) applied at different route classes. So one trigger_id could cover \"无有效会话\" for both. OK, not a real issue.\n\n   But here's a REAL issue I should check: §7 says \"POST /cliworker/select ... request with view:'full' or compat:'draft' without valid session → 403 E_VIEW_FORBIDDEN\". And §7 CLI: \"HTTP 上没有 schema 也视为 draft，所以同样需要会话\". Wait — \"同样需要会话\"?? Let me re-read: \"CLI 的 draft（没有 schema 或带 compat:'draft'）照常允许，并带 W_DRAFT_DEPRECATED。HTTP 上没有 schema 也视为 draft，所以同样需要会话。\" \n\n   Hmm! This says HTTP requests without schema (draft) \"同样需要会话\" (also require a session). But the route table says POST /cliworker/select does NOT require a session (AgentLine level), and only view:full/compat:draft without session → E_VIEW_FORBIDDEN. And §7 CLI section says CLI draft is allowed with W_DRAFT_DEPRECATED. So for HTTP: draft (no schema) requires a session? That seems to contradict \"POST /cliworker/select 不需要会话\". \n\n   Let me parse more carefully: \"CLI 的 draft（没有 schema 或带 compat:'draft'）照常允许\" — CLI draft allowed. \"HTTP 上没有 schema 也视为 draft，所以同样需要会话\" — on HTTP, no schema is also treated as draft, so it ALSO needs a session [like view:full / compat:draft]. Hmm, \"同样需要会话\" refers back to the earlier statement about view:full/compat:draft needing a session. So: on HTTP, draft requests (including no-schema) require a session; without a session → E_VIEW_FORBIDDEN. But the route table says \"POST /cliworker/select 不需要会话；请求带 view:'full' 或 compat:'draft'，而没有有效会话时返回 403 E_VIEW_FORBIDDEN\". The route table only mentions view:full/compat:draft, not \"no schema\". And §0.8 / AC-059 says: \"POST /cliworker/select 无 view 返回 200 AgentLine 视图且 scan 0 命中；POST select 带 view:'full' 或 compat:'draft' 无会话返回 403 E_VIEW_FORBIDDEN\". A request with \"no schema\" and no view — would that be \"无 view\"? If no schema means draft, and draft requires session, then \"无 view\" (no view field) but no schema → draft → E_VIEW_FORBIDDEN? But AC-059 says \"POST /cliworker/select 无 view 返回 200 AgentLine 视图\". Contradiction!\n\n   Hmm, this is subtle. Let me think. The design says: \"HTTP 上没有 schema 也视为 draft，所以同样需要会话\". But AC-059's first row: \"POST /cliworker/select 无 view 返回 200 AgentLine 视图且 scan 0 命中\". Does \"无 view\" include \"no schema\"? If the request has no `schema` field, it's draft, which \"需要会话\". So a no-view, no-schema request would need a session and return E_VIEW_FORBIDDEN, contradicting \"无 view 返回 200\". \n\n   Hmm, but maybe \"同样需要会话\" means \"draft view requires session just like full view does\" — i.e., draft is a management-level view on HTTP. But then the AgentLine default (no view, no compat) — what is the schema? The AgentLine request has a `schema` field? §7's AgentLine request fields: level, count, usage, importance, require_readonly, cwd, timeout_ms, request_id, exclude_handles — no `schema` field! So AgentLine requests inherently have no schema → treated as draft → require session → E_VIEW_FORBIDDEN. That would break the entire AgentLine flow!\n\n   Wait, this could be a real contradiction. Let me re-read §7 CLI section carefully:\n\n   \"**CLI：**\n   - `models select` 带 `view:'full'` 时返回 `E_VIEW_FORBIDDEN`；完整视图只由 `admin select` 提供。\n   - CLI 的 draft（没有 schema 或带 `compat:'draft'`）照常允许，并带 `W_DRAFT_DEPRECATED`。\n   - HTTP 上没有 schema 也视为 draft，所以同样需要会话。\n   - HTTP 响应和 CLI 输出都由 `lib/contract/scan.mjs` 检查。\"\n\n   Hmm, \"HTTP 上没有 schema 也视为 draft，所以同样需要会话\" — \"也\" (also) comparing to CLI draft. CLI draft is \"照常允许\" (allowed as usual). So \"同样\" might mean \"same as CLI\" = allowed? No, \"需要会话\" (needs session) — that's not \"allowed\". Confusing. Actually maybe it means: since HTTP treats no-schema as draft, and draft (like full view) needs a session, so HTTP no-schema also needs a session. \n\n   But this directly conflicts with the route table \"POST /cliworker/select 不需要会话\" and AC-059 \"无 view 返回 200\". This is a genuine internal contradiction in F-07/F-08's resolution! Let me double-check by re-reading §0.7 and AC-059, AC-056.\n\n   §0.7: \"GET /cliworker/api/session 和 GET /cliworker/ui ... 是路由表里显式列出的例外：不要求会话，只校验 loopback\". No mention of select draft.\n\n   AC-056: \"CLI 的 compat:draft 返回 draft 形状并带 W_DRAFT_DEPRECATED\" — CLI only.\n\n   AC-059: \"POST /cliworker/select 无 view 返回 200 AgentLine 视图且 scan 0 命中；POST select 带 view:'full' 或 compat:'draft' 无会话返回 403 E_VIEW_FORBIDDEN；有效会话时返回 200 完整或 draft 视图\". So on HTTP, `compat:'draft'` without session → 403; with session → 200 draft view. And \"无 view\" → 200 AgentLine. But a request with `compat:'draft'` is \"带 view\"? No, compat isn't view. Hmm, AC-059 says \"POST select 带 view:'full' 或 compat:'draft' 无会话返回 403\". OK so compat:draft no session → 403. And \"无 view 返回 200\" — presumably a plain request with neither view nor compat. If plain request has no schema → is it \"draft\"? If yes, then per §7 \"HTTP 上没有 schema 也视为 draft，所以同样需要会话\" → would need session → 403, contradicting \"无 view 返回 200\".\n\n   So there IS a tension. But is it a real blocker or an ambiguity? The design seems to intend: AgentLine plain select (no view, no compat) → 200 AgentLine view; view:full or compat:draft → needs session. The \"no schema also treated as draft\" line is about... hmm. Actually maybe I'm misreading Chinese. \"HTTP 上没有 schema 也视为 draft，所以同样需要会话\" — maybe \"同样\" refers to \"与 CLI 的 draft 一样\"? But CLI draft is allowed, not needing session. So \"需要会话\" doesn't match. Unless the intended meaning is: CLI draft is allowed (with warning), but on HTTP, no-schema is treated as draft, so [unlike CLI] it needs a session... \n\n   Hmm, this line is genuinely confusing and potentially contradictory. It's a candidate finding I found myself. But is it strong enough? Let me consider: the F-08 finding was about \"每个触发条件只对应一个错误码\"; this draft/session ambiguity reintroduces ambiguity into the trigger table. And F-07 was about session bootstrap. \n\n   Actually, wait. Let me reconsider. Maybe there's a deliberate design: AgentLine requests use a `schema` field set to the AgentLine schema (e.g., `cliworker.request/v1`), so they DO have a schema, hence not draft. \"没有 schema\" means literally missing the schema field. §7's AgentLine request list doesn't include schema, but maybe the schema is implied by the endpoint. Hmm. The design says \"HTTP 上没有 schema 也视为 draft\" — so a request without a schema field is draft. If AgentLine requests don't carry schema, they'd all be draft → need session → broken. But AC-059's first row says plain select returns 200. So either AgentLine requests carry a schema, or the \"no schema → draft\" rule doesn't apply to plain select. The design doesn't clarify. This is a real ambiguity worth flagging, but maybe at \"major\" or \"minor\". Hmm.\n\n   Actually, I realize I should be careful not to over-reach. Let me focus on what's clearly defensible and grounded. The 6 TBs are well-documented and clearly valid. Let me also consider whether to dispute any.\n\nLet me re-examine each TB for potential \"disputed\" status (i.e., I disagree it's a real blocker):\n\n- TB-1: The \"opposite conclusions\" claim — I argued heal