n- TB-6 (ac-index ownership contradiction): concrete doc contradiction. major? It's a one-line fix but violates single-source-of-truth. I'd say major.\n- TB-1 (clock file semantics): major.\n\nHmm, that gives 1 blocking + 5 major. Maybe I should make TB-2 and TB-5 blocking (both are oracle-integrity holes). Let me think about what \"blocking\" means: prevents implementation / must be fixed before proceeding. All 6 are open issues that need fixing before implementation. But severity distinguishes.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: the verdict is \"revise\" — meaning the designer must revise. All open findings need addressing. Severity is about how critical. I'll assign:\n- F-01 (TB-1): blocking — clock semantics foundational, affects AC-028/029/061/064/072 and health; env leakage can corrupt many tests.\n\nHmm, actually let me not overthink. Let me settle on a reasonable distribution and make sure each finding's text is precise, cites the concrete contradiction, and states what's needed. The text quality matters most.\n\nLet me also decide about including a \"resolved\" catch-all finding. In review-v2, F-09 (resolved) acknowledged verification of 勘误 and prior findings. I think including one is good practice: it shows I verified F-01..F-08 and found the core resolutions sound, and that my revise is scoped to the 6 residual gaps. That adds credibility. I'll include a final finding with status \"resolved\" noting the 8 findings' core resolutions are verified (with specifics), and that the revise is limited to the 6 TB-derived gaps. \n\nBut careful: if I mark a finding \"resolved\" with text acknowledging F-01..F-08, but I also have 6 open findings that are residual gaps in those same F-0x fixes, that's consistent: \"core resolution accepted; residual gaps listed above.\" Good.\n\nNow, ID scheme: I'll use F-01..F-06 for the 6 confirmed TBs (mapping TB-1→F-01, ..., TB-6→F-06), plus F-07 as the resolved catch-all. Hmm, but that leaves no room for my own new findings. Do I ha